// Deliberately carries no EXPECT marker, for the same reason as
// 41_reference_reject.adl and its siblings: this is correctly rejected
// (see the printed "Cannot add new content to a complete object"
// diagnostic below), but like every other semantic check that isn't yet
// wired through to fail the parse, that's print-only and doesn't
// currently affect the exit code. Mark this test properly once errors
// are propagated through a proper ErrNum (see cpp/ToDo).
//
// adl.adl's own comment on Is Complete: "No further contents may be
// added." Widget declares two fields first, then becomes complete.
// Size is declared plain (": Reference;"), not via "-> Object" shorthand
// - that shorthand's own implicit default-restriction assignment
// (README "Reference Shorthand") would already count as Size's "first"
// local Assignment, made before completeness, muddying this example.
Widget: {
	Colour: String;
	Size: Reference;
}
Widget { Is Complete = True; }

// Faulty: Widget is now complete, so Weight may not be added as a new
// field.
Widget {
	Weight: String;
}

// Faulty: a variable's very first assignment is new content (a brand new
// Assignment), even though Colour itself was declared before Widget
// became complete.
Widget {
	Colour = 'Red';
}

// Faulty for the same reason - and an object-literal value can only ever
// attach to a new Assignment (there's nothing existing to attach to), so
// this is rejected the same way, not treated specially.
Widget {
	Size = :Object{};
}
